re already known, I'm really predicting how a skeptical reviewer would interpret them, or what gaps would emerge if we pushed the experiments further.\n\nThe user is asking me to generate detailed predicted results for each planned experiment under the worst-tier narrative—essentially imagining what the data would show if the paper's core claims don't hold up as strongly as presented. I need to provide rationales for each prediction that align with the worst-tier perspective.\n\nThe key insight is that the worst-tier predictions should acknowledge the real positive results we've already observed, but frame them as less compelling through specific critiques: the findings are unsurprising, lack practical utility given the false positive rates, involve excessive hyperparameter tuning, rely on limited samples, have model-specific caveats, and don't justify the engineering overhead of white-box access. So I'm generating predictions that show the actual positive results but with explanations for why a skeptical reviewer would find them unconvincing.\n\nNow I'm drafting the full YAML structure covering all 15 planned experiments plus the what_if ensemble variant, thinking through how each experiment's worst-tier prediction should present real but uncompelling results given the paper's current state. Now I'm cross-referencing the planned experiments against what's actually in the registry to see which ones have been completed and which are still theoretical. Most of the early experiments have corresponding entries—the window ablation, cross-transfer, and various statistical validation approaches are all done—but a few like exp-009 and exp-010 don't show up in the records yet, so those might be genuinely unrun or planned for later.\n\nFor the worst-tier scenario, I need to generate predicted metrics for all these experiments that tell a coherent story of pessimistic but plausible results, then format everything as a complete YAML matching the dream report structure with specific numerical predictions that stay internally consistent with the narrative that reviewer reception would be poor. than experimental failure, I should predict results that broadly match what's known but highlight the aspects that make reviewers skeptical:\n\n1. Safe FPR remains 39-62% across all methods → \"useless monitor\"\n2. R1-32B: W=1 only significant, 25% coverage → unconvincing\n3. QwQ: negative HS lead time → text detects earlier\n4. Position mediation explains much of the HS advantage\n5. Small n (19-20) for 32B → underpowered for strong claims\n6. Layer selection sensitivity = \"you just tuned harder for HS\"\n\nFor the ensemble under worst-case review, the cross-validation p-value doesn't overcome fundamental objections about framing—the skepticism centers on methodology, not statistical significance.\n\nNow I'm working through the metric predictions for each experiment. The layer sweep across architectures shows biased selection that reviewers will frame as hyperparameter tuning rather than discovery, with significant HS advantages only under specific metric choices. For the temporal window analysis, R1-32B gains are modest at best while QwQ shows robustness, though coverage actually drops as the window widens.\n\nCross-model transfer completely fails, confirming that safety encoding is model-specific and undermining any generalizability claims. Lead time results are mixed—QwQ shows negative HS lead time while R1-32B only achieves positive lead time at W=1 with just 25% coverage, making it impractical for deployment. The Mahalanobis distance approach fails across all four models.\n\nBootstrap confidence intervals pass for all 12 HB cases with corrected layers, though the FPR p-values are marginal for both R1-32B and QwQ, and the intervals are quite wide for the 32B model given the small sample sizes. Window ablation shows the expected precision-sensitivity tradeoff—coverage decreases as W increases, with R1-8B performing best at W=25 and the larger models peaking at different window sizes, but this is standard machine learning behavior rather than novel.\n\nLayer selection ablation reveals a crossing_rate bias that, once corrected, demonstrates HS advantage, though a reviewer would likely dismiss this as mere hyperparameter tuning rather than a scientific contribution. The 16-label taxonomy experiment isn't clearly documented in the registry and only yields marginal improvements. The oracle decomposition exposes a fundamental gap between what's detectable and what's present in hidden states, but this finding feels somewhat expected. Adding OpenThinker-7B to the pipeline produces strong results across all three HB cases with good selectivity, yet critics would argue that validating another 7B model doesn't resolve the lingering concerns about 32B performance.\n\nFor the GRU experiments on 32B, I need to re-evaluate the R1-32B results at layer 63 and incorporate the QwQ findings—though the marginal gains over logistic regression just reinforce that the underlying signal is straightforward. The text baseline shows a substantial 15-24pp precision advantage across all four models, but the white-box requirement makes it impractical compared to zero-infrastructure text probes. BOCPD fails almost entirely except on R1-8B where it reaches 93%, suggesting advanced change-point detection methods don't generalize well to this problem.\n\nThe false positive rate across all methods sits at 39-62%, which is the critical issue—a monitor firing on nearly half of safe traces is fundamentally unreliable. The ensemble cross-validation confirms statistical significance at p≤0.002, but the actual acceptance probability improvement is only 0-3%, so it doesn't bridge the gap from worst to median performance tier. Now I'm composing the YAML structure to capture these actual results alongside the worst-tier reviewer's interpretation of why each approach falls short.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details"